0 8 8 8 0 / 8 8 0 8 8`) is symmetric. The 2, 1, 7 and 6 shapes are not.\n- **Pair 3:** the 6 shape (`6 6 0 6 6 / 0 6 6 6 0 / 0 6 0 6 0`) is symmetric. The 3, 1, 7 and 4 shapes are not.\n\nOne detail: a single shape can split into several connected pieces, such as the stray single 3s in pair 1 or the loose 8 dots in pair 2. So I group cells by colour, not by connected component. Each colour's bounding box is one candidate.\n\n**Rule:** for each colour, crop its bounding box. Keep only that colour's cells and set every other cell to background. Output the one crop that equals its own left-right mirror. If none or several qualify, the code falls back to the largest symmetric candidate, or the largest overall.\n\n**Test input:**\n- The 1 shape fails on its top row: `0 0 1 1 0` is not a mirror of itself.\n- The 4 shape fails at row 17.\n- The 8 shape fails at its bottom row: `0 8 0 0 0`.\n- The 2 shape (x 7–15, y 6–11) is symmetric about column 11 on every row.\n\nSo the answer is the 6×9 crop of the 2 shape.\n\n**Emit stages:**\n1. An emit showing the chosen shape.\n2. An emit of the final cropped canvas.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    cands = []\n    for c in np.unique(g):\n        if c == bg:\n            continue\n        cells = list(zip(*np.where(g == c)))\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(cells)\n        sub = crop(g, y0, x0, y1, x1)\n        sub = np.where(sub == c, c, bg)\n        sym = np.array_equal(sub, sub[:, ::-1])\n        cands.append((sym, len(cells), int(c), sub, (y0, x0, y1, x1)))\n    sym_c = [t for t in cands if t[0]]\n    pool = sym_c if sym_c else cands\n    best = max(pool, key=lambda t: t[1])\n    sym, n, c, sub, (y0, x0, y1, x1) = best\n    marked = np.full_like(g, bg)\n    marked[y0:y1 + 1, x0:x1 + 1] = sub\n    emit(marked, f\"Colour {c} is the only shape that is mirror-symmetric left-right; isolate it.\")\n    out = sub.copy()\n    emit(out, f\"Crop the colour-{c} shape to its bounding box: that is the output.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"If none or several qualify, the code falls back to the largest symmetric candidate, or the largest overall."}],"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**How the chosen shape differs from the others.** It is the only shape that is mirror-symmetric left-to-right."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 2:** the 8 shape (`0 8 0 8 0 / 0 0 8 0 0 / 0 8 8 8 0 / 8 8 0 8 8`) is symmetric."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 3:** the 6 shape (`6 6 0 6 6 / 0 6 6 6 0 / 0 6 0 6 0`) is symmetric."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"If none or several qualify, the code falls back to the largest symmetric candidate, or the largest overall."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"In each training pair, the output is exactly one of them, cut out to its bounding box with the background kept around it."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each colour's bounding box is one candidate."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Rule:** for each colour, crop its bounding box."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"c3275a02671966c42ed93bfa82f8df2d34514a8ce31d8404d8c7acc070da6e27","task_sha256":"46a2d25e1b619382affe98e5405df32aefbe589cc97c3b7a97f6c0b2ce1c24c6","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
